an class="block truncate text-body-medium font-medium text-sys-on-surface">What are invitation sentences in Japanese?</span></span><span class="peel-symbol shrink-0 text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></div><div class="article-content min-w-0"><div><section class="section"><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">To invite the listener to join the speaker in doing a certain action, we can use invitation sentences called <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"> 勧誘文</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>kan’yūbun</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> in Japanese. You can form a sentence like this using one of three possible structures:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class=""><div class="relative w-full overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full caption-bottom border-collapse text-base [&amp;_th]:h-10 [&amp;_th]:px-4 [&amp;_th]:py-2 [&amp;_td]:px-4 [&amp;_td]:py-3 table-auto"><caption class="mb-4 text-left text-sm text-muted-foreground"></caption><thead class=""><tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50 data-[state=selected]:bg-muted bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800"><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1">Invitation Structure</th><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1">Endings<!-- --></th></tr></thead><tbody class="[&amp;_tr:last-child]:border-0"><tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50 data-[state=selected]:bg-muted"><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">the volitional form</p></div></td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span><br/><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></p></div></td></tr><tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50 data-[state=selected]:bg-muted"><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">the volitional form + <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">か</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></p></div></td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょうか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>mashō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span><br/><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)うか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></p></div></td></tr><tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50 data-[state=selected]:bg-muted"><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">a negative question</p></div></td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-left align-middle border-t border-r border-b border-l whitespace-nowrap" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"><div class="space-y-2"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span><br/><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">While all three can be used to invite something to do something, they are not always interchangeable. In this post, we will find out what invitation sentences are and how and when we can use each of the above expressions. Let’s get started or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">始めましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>hajimemashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>!</p></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="what-are-invitation-sentences-in-japanese">What are invitation sentences in Japanese?</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Invitation sentences, which are called <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">勧誘文</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>kanyūbun</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> in Japanese, are expressions that you can use to <strong class="font-semibold">invite someone to do something with you</strong>. There are two types of invitation sentences:</p><ol class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-decimal [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">“Let’s” type:</strong></p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">In this type, <strong class="font-semibold">the speaker and the listener are starting something as a team</strong>. The speaker encourages the listener to start the action together.</p><aside style="--cn-border:var(--mark-ink)" class="w-full rounded-r-md py-2 px-3 my-2 border-l-4 bg-sys-surface-container border-[var(--cn-border)]"><p class="text-xs leading-tight text-left not-italic no-underline normal-case mb-1 font-medium text-sys-on-surface-variant">Example:</p><div class="space-y-1">Everyone in the room is dancing except John and Yoko. John invites Yoko to dance together: “Let’s dance,” “Shall we dance?” or “Why don’t we dance?”</div></aside></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">“Join-me” type:</strong></p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">In this type, <strong class="font-semibold">the speaker is already doing something</strong>, and encourages the listener to join them. Only the listener will be changing their behavior.</p><aside style="--cn-border:var(--mark-ink)" class="w-full rounded-r-md py-2 px-3 my-2 border-l-4 bg-sys-surface-container border-[var(--cn-border)]"><p class="text-xs leading-tight text-left not-italic no-underline normal-case mb-1 font-medium text-sys-on-surface-variant">Example:</p><div class="space-y-1">John has been dancing with his friends, while Yoko is sitting at the table. John approaches Yoko and invites her to join him in dancing: “Won’t you dance too?” or “Why don’t you dance with me?” <!-- --></div></aside></li></ol><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">All three of the structures we mentioned in the introduction can be used to form both kinds of invitations, but it’s important to keep this distinction in mind, because it can impact how you’ll use them.</p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Let’s have a look!</p></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="invitations-with-y-or-mash">Invitations with 〜(よ)う [yō] or 〜ましょう [mashō]</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">One way to form invitations in Japanese is to use the endings <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜(よ)う</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">yō</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ましょう</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. This is most often used to form “let’s” invitations in a situation where the invitation is expected.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">In casual contexts</strong>, you’ll use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜う</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜よう</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">yō</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. These endings are used to create the volitional form of the verb, a form which is often used to express that you have decided or resolved to do something. </p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">行く → 行こ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">う</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>iku → ik<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ō</span></strong> <!-- --></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>go → let’s go<!-- --></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">見る → 見<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">よう</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>miru → mi<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">yō</span></strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>see → let’s see</div></div></li></ul><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Though volitional forms will all end with <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>, you may want to check out our post on the <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/the-volitional-form-how-to-state-intentions-or-suggest-actions-in-japanese-">volitional in Japanese</a> to learn the full rules for creating this form!</p></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">In polite contexts</strong>, you’ll use the ending <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ましょう</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. This is the volitional form of the ending <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ます</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masu</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>, so you’ll just start with the polite form and use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> to replace the ending.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">行き<strong class="font-semibold">ます</strong> → 行き<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ましょう</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>iki<strong class="font-semibold">masu</strong> → iki<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">mashō</span></strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>go → let’s go</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">見<strong class="font-semibold">ます</strong> → 見<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ましょう</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>mi<strong class="font-semibold">masu</strong> → mi<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">mashō</span></strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>see → let’s see</div></div></li></ul><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-primary"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-primary text-sys-on-primary"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Tip</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">The volitional form has several other uses as well. For example, it can be used to express that the speaker has a strong will or resolution to do something or it can be used to offer a service. To determine whether it’s used to express volition or invitation, look at who the “doer” is:</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">If the doer of the action is <strong class="font-semibold">the speaker (“I”)</strong>, it&#x27;s a <strong class="font-semibold">volitional </strong>sentence:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">その荷物、<strong class="font-semibold">私が</strong>運びましょう。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sono nimotsu, <strong class="font-semibold">watashi ga</strong> hakobimashō.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span><strong class="font-semibold">I’ll </strong>carry that baggage for you.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">If the doers of the action are <strong class="font-semibold">the speaker and the listener (“we”)</strong>, it’s an invitation sentence:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">その荷物、<strong class="font-semibold">いっしょに</strong>運びましょう。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sono nimotsu, <strong class="font-semibold">issho ni</strong> hakobimashō.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span><strong class="font-semibold">Let’s</strong> carry that baggage together.</div></div></div></div></li></ul><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Please note that the doer of the action in invitation sentences is often omitted and only implied. In those cases, we need to pay attention to the contextual cues, such as <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">いっしょに</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>issho ni</span><span aria-hidden="true">, </span><span class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">meaning </span><span aria-hidden="true">“</span>together<span aria-hidden="true">”</span></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> in the second example above.<!-- --></p></div></div></aside></li></ul><section class="section"><h3 class="text-balance text-headline-small text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="when-to-use-mash-or-y-for-invitations">When to use 〜ましょう [-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō] for invitations?</h3><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">As we mentioned above, <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜 (よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> are most typically used to form “let’s” type invitations: to invite someone else to start an action at the same time you do. Specifically, you’ll use these endings in a situation where you’re <strong class="font-semibold">pretty sure the other person will accept</strong>, because you’ve made a prior agreement, because there’s a general expectation, or because you and your listener habitually do the action.</p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Let’s look at some examples!</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Field trip chaperone:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">みんな、いるね？じゃあ、行こ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">う</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Min’na iru ne? Jā, ik<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ō</span></strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Everyone is here, right? Well then, let’s go.</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">There is an expectation or agreement that the kids will accept the invitation</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Store Manager:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">では、今日も笑顔でがんばり<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ましょう</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Dewa, kyō mo egao de ganbari<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">mashō</span></strong>. <!-- --></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Well then, as usual, let’s do our best today with smiles on our faces.<!-- --></div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">The employees habitually do this, so it’s expected that they’ll accept the invitation.<!-- --></div></div></div></li></ul><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">You <strong class="font-semibold">can </strong>use these two endings to form an invitation when there is no clear expectation of acceptance, but in this case it can sound a little pushy, like you’re not really giving the listener an opportunity to say “no.”</p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">For example, imagine it’s the last night before the end of summer break, and the kids had expected to sleep in…</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div><div class="flex flex-col gap-3"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Parent:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">明日は早く起きて、ハイキングに行こ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">う</span></strong>！</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ashita wa hayaku okite, haikingu ni ik<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ō</span></strong>!</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Let’s get up early tomorrow and go hiking!</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Doesn’t really give the kids the chance to say “no.”</div></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Children:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">えっ〜！</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ē!</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Whaaat?!</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-primary"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-primary text-sys-on-primary"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Tip</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">You can sometimes use these endings to form <strong class="font-semibold">“join-me” type</strong> invitations. These, though, can sound quite pushy, so it’s usually only done among close friends and family members.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Kenta is playing baseball with his friends in a neighborhood park. He sees his neighbor’s son Akira walking by. Kenta invites Akira to join the game.</p><div><div class="flex flex-col gap-3"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Kenta:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">あきらくんも野球し<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">よう</span></strong>！</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Akira kun mo yakyū shi<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">yō</span></strong>!</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Let’s play baseball, Akira! </div></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Akira:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">やる、やる！</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Yaru, yaru!</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Will do!</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></aside></div></section><section class="section"><h3 class="text-balance text-headline-small text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="how-to-soften-invitations-with-mash-or-y">How to soften invitations with 〜ましょう[-mashō] or 〜 (よ)う [-yō]?</h3><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">If you don’t want to sound quite so pushy, you can soften the tone of an invitation with <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜 (よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> by adding particles to the end of the sentence.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Add the <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/yo-and-ne-how-to-use-these-sentence-ending-particles-in-japanese-#how-to-use-yo">particle <span class="inline-translation"><span class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">よ</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">yo</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></a> at the end of the invitation to solicit agreement. For example, let’s imagine coworkers Yoko and Hikaru are deciding on where to have lunch:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Yoko:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">今日はこの店で食べ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ましょう</span></strong><strong class="font-semibold">よ</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kyō wa kono mise de tabe<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">mashō</span></strong><strong class="font-semibold"> yo</strong>.<!-- --></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Let’s eat at this place today, <strong class="font-semibold">shall we</strong>?<!-- --></div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Adding <!-- --><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">よ</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yo</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> gives Yoko a chance to agree or not.<!-- --></div></div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">To respond positively to an invitation like this, you can use a generic phrase <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">そうしましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>sō shimashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>, which literally means “Let’s do so.”</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Hikaru: </span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">いいですね。<strong class="font-semibold">そうしましょう</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ii desu ne. <strong class="font-semibold">Sō shimashō</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>It sounds good to me. <strong class="font-semibold">Let’s do so</strong>.</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Adding <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">よ</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yo</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> gives Yoko a chance to agree or not.</div></div></div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Add the <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/yo-and-ne-how-to-use-these-sentence-ending-particles-in-japanese-#how-to-use-ne">particle <span class="inline-translation"><span class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">ね</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">ne</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></a> at the end of an invitation sentence to give a gentle nudge, like when you end an English sentence with “OK?” In this case, the intonation goes up at <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ね</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>ne</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. </p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">This is usually <strong class="font-semibold">not used between peers or to a superior</strong>!</p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">The ending combinations <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)うね</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-yō ne</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ましょうね</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō ne</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>, are often used by parents or other nurturing figures, to give gentler, more roundabout commands or to persuade reluctant children:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Parent:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">暗くなってきたから、今日はもう帰ろ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">う</span></strong><strong class="font-semibold">ね</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kurakunatte kita kara, kyō wa mō kaer<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-blue) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ō</span></strong><strong class="font-semibold"> ne</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>It’s getting dark, so let’s go home now for today, <strong class="font-semibold">OK</strong>?</div></div></div></div></div></li></ul></div></section></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="invitations-with-mash-ka-or-y-ka">Invitations with 〜ましょうか [mashō ka] or 〜(よ)うか [yō ka]</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Another way to form an invitation is to phrase it as a question, by adding the <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/ka-kana-and-kke-how-to-use-these-japanese-sentence-ending-particles-">question particle <span class="inline-translation"><span class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span></a> after the volitional forms above, creating the endings <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ましょうか</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">mashō ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜 (よ)うか</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">yō ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. </p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="w-full"><div class="flex flex-col items-center md:flex-row md:flex-wrap md:justify-left gap-3"><div style="--eq-border:var(--mark-blue)" class="group/chunk relative isolate cursor-pointer rounded-md border-2 text-center transition-opacity duration-150 border-[var(--eq-border)] bg-sys-surface-container py-4 px-5 text-base"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Isolate part 1" data-isolate-control="" class="absolute z-10 grid place-items-center rounded-full border border-current/25 bg-sys-surface/70 backdrop-blur-[2px] transition-opacity duration-150 hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 top-1.5 left-1.5 size-5" style="opacity:0.45"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v2m0 14v2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4m10 10l1.4 1.4M3 12h2m14 0h2M5.6 18.4L7 17m10-10l1.4-1.4M12 9a3 3 0 100 6 3 3 0 000-6z"></path></svg></button><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left my-2 text-sm" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ましょう</strong> / <strong class="font-semibold">〜 (よ)う	</strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>-mashō / yō</div></div></div><div class="flex items-center justify-center text-muted-foreground"><span class="peel-symbol size-4 shrink-0" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></div><div style="--eq-border:var(--mark-crimson)" class="group/chunk relative isolate cursor-pointer rounded-md border-2 text-center transition-opacity duration-150 border-[var(--eq-border)] bg-sys-surface-container py-4 px-5 text-base"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Isolate part 2" data-isolate-control="" class="absolute z-10 grid place-items-center rounded-full border border-current/25 bg-sys-surface/70 backdrop-blur-[2px] transition-opacity duration-150 hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 top-1.5 left-1.5 size-5" style="opacity:0.45"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v2m0 14v2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4m10 10l1.4 1.4M3 12h2m14 0h2M5.6 18.4L7 17m10-10l1.4-1.4M12 9a3 3 0 100 6 3 3 0 000-6z"></path></svg></button><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><span class="inline-translation"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span><br/>question particle</p></div></div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">You’ll use these endings to form “Let’s…” type commands that give the listener more opportunity to decline. English equivalents might be <strong class="font-semibold">“Shall we…?”</strong> or <strong class="font-semibold">“Do you want to…?”</strong>, because they are technically asking the listener what they want to do. As a result, invitations phrased as questions sound gentler and more polite. </p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">However, <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょうか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>mashō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)うか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> still sound most natural in situations where the speaker and the listener<strong class="font-semibold"> already generally agree</strong> that they’re going to do something, but might disagree on the <strong class="font-semibold">details</strong>.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Coworkers Tanaka and Suzuki are told by their boss to go to Osaka this afternoon to meet their client there. They are quickly making a travel plan.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Tanaka:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">大阪へは２時の新幹線で行き<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">か</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ōsaka e wa ni ji no shinkansen de iki<strong class="font-semibold">mashō </strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ka</span></strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Shall we go to Osaka by a 2:00-pm shinkansen?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Suzuki:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ええ、そうしましょう。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ē, sō shimashō.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Yes, let’s do so.</div></div></div></li></ul></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">You can also use <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/how-to-ask-questions-in-japanese-">question words</a> to ask your listener a question about a shared plan.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-0.5 mb-2"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));border-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 40%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer rounded-sm border px-2 py-0.5 font-medium transition-opacity duration-150 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary text-sm">question word</button></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-3"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Tanaka:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">今夜は<span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150"><strong class="font-semibold">どこ</strong></span>に泊まり<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">か</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kon’ya wa <span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150"><strong class="font-semibold">doko</strong></span> ni tomari<strong class="font-semibold">mashō </strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ka</span></strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-magenta) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150"><strong class="font-semibold">Where</strong></span> shall we stay tonight?</div></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Suzuki: </span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">マンゴーホテルに泊まり<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong>。安くて便利ですから。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Mangō hoteru ni tomari<strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong>. Yasukute benri desu kara.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Let’s stay at the Mango Hotel. It’s inexpensive and convenient.</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">You can also use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)うか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょうか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> to remind the listener to do something they were planning to do already.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example:</strong> Tanaka and Suzuki are getting ready to leave the office to catch the 2:00 shinkansen.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Suzuki:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">もう１時ですね。そろそろ出かけ<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">か</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Mō ichi ji desu ne. Sorosoro dekake <strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong> <strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ka</span></strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>It’s already 1:00, isn’t it? Shall we leave soon?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Tanaka:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ええ、そうしましょう。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ē, sō shimashō.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Yes, let’s do so.</div></div></div></li></ul></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">In literal questions, the question marker <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> is often dropped, to soften the tone, especially when the speaker is female. However, you should<strong class="font-semibold"> never omit </strong><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> from a casual invitation, regardless of your gender or the level of formality.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>のど、かわいたね。なんか飲も<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">か</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Nodo, kawaita ne. Nanka nom<strong class="font-semibold">ō </strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ka</span></strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>We are thirsty, aren’t we? Shall we get something to drink?</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span>のど、かわいたね。なんか飲もう<strong class="font-semibold">か</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Nodo, kawaita ne. Nanka nom<strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong>?</div></div></li></ul><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-error"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-error text-sys-on-error"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Important</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Please note that we <strong class="font-semibold">cannot </strong>use these to form <strong class="font-semibold">“Join-me” type</strong> invitations. See the example below:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Yoko has been swimming while John is sun-bathing on the poolside. Yoko approaches John and invites him to join her in the water.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>  ジョンも泳ご<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Jon mo oyog<strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Let’s swim, John.</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span>  ジョンも泳ご<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">か</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Jon mo oyog<strong class="font-semibold">ō </strong><strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ka</span></strong>.</div></div></li></ul></div></div></div></div></aside></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="invitations-with-masen-ka-and-nai-ka">Invitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] and 〜ないか [nai ka]</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Finally, it’s possible to form an invitation in Japanese using a negative question. The English equivalents would be <strong class="font-semibold">“Won’t you…?”</strong> or <strong class="font-semibold">“Why don’t we…?”</strong></p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="w-full"><div class="flex flex-col items-center md:flex-row md:flex-wrap md:justify-left gap-3"><div style="--eq-border:var(--mark-crimson)" class="group/chunk relative isolate cursor-pointer rounded-md border-2 text-center transition-opacity duration-150 border-[var(--eq-border)] bg-sys-surface-container py-4 px-5 text-base"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Isolate part 1" data-isolate-control="" class="absolute z-10 grid place-items-center rounded-full border border-current/25 bg-sys-surface/70 backdrop-blur-[2px] transition-opacity duration-150 hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 top-1.5 left-1.5 size-5" style="opacity:0.45"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v2m0 14v2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4m10 10l1.4 1.4M3 12h2m14 0h2M5.6 18.4L7 17m10-10l1.4-1.4M12 9a3 3 0 100 6 3 3 0 000-6z"></path></svg></button><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">Negative verb</strong><br/>plain or polite form</p></div><div class="flex items-center justify-center text-muted-foreground"><span class="peel-symbol size-4 shrink-0" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></div><div style="--eq-border:var(--mark-ink)" class="group/chunk relative isolate cursor-pointer rounded-md border-2 text-center transition-opacity duration-150 border-[var(--eq-border)] bg-sys-surface-container py-4 px-5 text-base"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Isolate part 2" data-isolate-control="" class="absolute z-10 grid place-items-center rounded-full border border-current/25 bg-sys-surface/70 backdrop-blur-[2px] transition-opacity duration-150 hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 top-1.5 left-1.5 size-5" style="opacity:0.45"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v2m0 14v2M5.6 5.6l1.4 1.4m10 10l1.4 1.4M3 12h2m14 0h2M5.6 18.4L7 17m10-10l1.4-1.4M12 9a3 3 0 100 6 3 3 0 000-6z"></path></svg></button><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><span class="inline-translation"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span><br/>question particle</p></div></div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Because the <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/what-are-the-verb-groups-in-japanese-">plain negative form</a> generally ends in <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ない</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> while <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/what-are-the-verb-groups-in-japanese-">polite negative verbs<!-- --></a> end in <!-- --><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ません</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>, invitations following this patter generally end in <!-- --><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ないか</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">-nai ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <!-- --><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ませんか</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">-masen ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>.<!-- --></p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Casual:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-0.5 mb-0.5"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));border-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 40%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer rounded-sm border px-2 py-0.5 font-medium transition-opacity duration-150 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary text-sm">plain negative verb</button></div><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150">行か<strong class="font-semibold">ない</strong></span><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150">ika<strong class="font-semibold">nai</strong></span> <strong class="font-semibold">ka</strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t we go?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Polite:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-0.5 mb-0.5"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));border-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 40%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer rounded-sm border px-2 py-0.5 font-medium transition-opacity duration-150 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-sys-primary text-sm">polite negative verb</button></div><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150">行き<strong class="font-semibold">ません</strong></span><strong class="font-semibold">か</strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 84%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface));background:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 8%, transparent);border-radius:0.1875rem;padding:0.25em 0.2em;margin:-0.25em 0;-webkit-tap-highlight-color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 30%, transparent);opacity:1;text-decoration-line:underline;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:from-font;text-underline-offset:0.2em" class="cursor-pointer transition-[opacity,color] duration-150">iki<strong class="font-semibold">masen</strong></span><strong class="font-semibold"> ka</strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t we go?</div></div></div></li></ul><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">When an invitation is given as a negative question, the question marker <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">か</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> <strong class="font-semibold">can </strong>be omitted in casual contexts to soften the tone, as long as your intonation goes up at the end of the sentence.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">疲れたね。ちょっと休憩し<strong class="font-semibold">ない</strong>？</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Tsukareta ne. Chotto kyūkei shi<strong class="font-semibold">nai</strong>?</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Tired, aren’t we? Why don’t we take five?</div></div></div></div><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-primary"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-primary text-sys-on-primary"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Tip</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">When you use a negative question to form an invitation, you’re literally asking someone what their intentions are. So when you hear a question like this, need to use context to tell whether someone is inviting you to do something or just asking you a question. Compare:</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">Question:</strong></p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">The speaker picks up the cup beside the listener and sees there is a small amount of stale coffee in it.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">これ、飲み<strong class="font-semibold">ませんか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kore, nomi<strong class="font-semibold">masen ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Are you drinking this?</div><div class="literal italic text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Literally: </span><span aria-hidden="true">lit. </span>Will you not drink this?</div></div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><strong class="font-semibold">Invitation:</strong></p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">The speaker brings a fresh cup of coffee to the listener.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">これ、飲み<strong class="font-semibold">ませんか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kore, nomi<strong class="font-semibold">masen ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Would you like to drink this?</div><div class="literal italic text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Literally: </span><span aria-hidden="true">lit. </span>Will you not drink this?</div></div></div></div></li></ul></div></div></aside><section class="section"><h3 class="text-balance text-headline-small text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="when-to-use-masen-ka-and-nai-ka-for-invitations">When to use 〜ませんか [-masen ka] and 〜ないか [-nai ka] for invitations?</h3><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">You can use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> in both <strong class="font-semibold">“Let’s” type </strong>and <strong class="font-semibold">“Join-me” type</strong> invitations. They are considered more polite than either of the earlier two patterns we discussed. Here are, then, some of the cases where you’re more likely to hear negative questions used to issue invitations:</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">When issuing a <strong class="font-semibold">“join-me” type</strong> invitation, a negative question is your best option. This is especially true in more polite contexts or with less familiar people.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example:</strong> After an office party, Tanaka and Yamada are getting into a taxi to head to the second venue. They see Sato approaching and invite her to share the ride.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Tanaka:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">佐藤さんも乗りませんか。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Satō san mo norimasen ka.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t you ride with us, Ms. Sato?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Sato:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">あ、乗ります。どうもありがとう。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>A, norimasu. Dōmo arigatō.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Oh, I will. Thank you very much.</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"> Often a positive acceptance will also end in <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">-ます</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masu</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>.</div></div></div></li></ul><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-primary"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-primary text-sys-on-primary"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Tip</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">In this case, the invitation could be issued using just the volitional, though it would sound pushy or very familiar.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="ok">🆗</span>佐藤さんも乗り<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Satō san mo nori<strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong>.</div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">But you <strong class="font-semibold">cannot </strong>issue this type of invitation using a positive question.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span>佐藤さんも乗り<strong class="font-semibold">ましょうか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Satō san mo nori<strong class="font-semibold">mashō ka</strong>.</div></div></div></div></div></div></aside></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">When issuing a <strong class="font-semibold">first </strong>or <strong class="font-semibold">potentially unexpected </strong>invitation, using a negative question can show respect for the listener’s desires. </p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">This especially applies to <strong class="font-semibold">“join-me”</strong> <strong class="font-semibold">type</strong> invitations.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Kimura and his coworkers have been planning a social event. They want to include Tom, who is new to the office, but not sure if he is interested.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"> Kimura:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">金曜日にお花見をするんですけど、トムさんも参加し<strong class="font-semibold">ませんか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Kin’yōbi ni ohanami o suru n desu kedo, Tomu san mo sanka shi<strong class="font-semibold">masen ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>We are having a cherry-blossom-viewing picnic on Friday. Won’t you join us too, Tom?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Tom:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">お花見？いいですね！ぜひ参加させてください。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ohanami? Ii desu ne! Zehi sanka sasete kudasai.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Cherry-blossom viewing? Sounds good! Let me join you by all means.</div></div></div></li></ul></div></div><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-error"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-error text-sys-on-error"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Important</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">This “join-me” invitation <strong class="font-semibold">cannot </strong>be formed using a positive question:<!-- --></p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span>トムさんも参加しましょ<strong class="font-semibold">うか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Tomu san mo sanka shimash<strong class="font-semibold">ō ka</strong>.</div></div></div></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">It also <strong class="font-semibold">would not generally </strong>be formed using just the volitional, since that would sound pushy.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="ok">🆗</span>トムさんも参加しましょ<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Tomu san mo sanka shimash<strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong>.</div></div></div></div></div></div></aside><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">However it can also be used in<strong class="font-semibold"> “let’s” type </strong>invitations, as below.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Michiko wants to invite her new friend Satsuki to go hiking, but not sure if Satsuki is willing to do so.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Michiko: </span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">明日ひまなら、いっしょにハイキングに行かない？</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Ashita hima nara, issho ni haikingu ni ikanai?</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>If you are free tomorrow, why don’t we go hiking together?</div></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Satsuki: </span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ハイキング？うん、いいよ。<strong class="font-semibold">行こう</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Haikingu? Un, ii yo. <strong class="font-semibold">Ikō</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Hiking? Sure. <strong class="font-semibold">Let’s go</strong>.</div></div></div></li></ul></div></div></li></ul><aside class="text-sys-on-surface flex gap-3.5 rounded-lg border p-4 border-sys-error"><span class="grid size-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-sys-error text-sys-on-error"><span class="peel-symbol" style="font-size:20px;font-variation-settings:&quot;FILL&quot; 0, &quot;wght&quot; 400, &quot;GRAD&quot; 0, &quot;opsz&quot; 20" translate="no" aria-hidden="true"></span></span><div class="min-w-0"><div class="text-body-large [&amp;&gt;*+*]:mt-3"><div class="mb-1.5 flex items-center gap-2"><span class="text-title-small text-sys-on-surface">Important</span></div><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Even though <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> is a more polite form of invitation, it is still grammatically familiar, so it is most fitting when the listener is socially equal to the speaker.  If inviting a superior to do something, the humble request form <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><strong class="font-semibold">〜ていただけませんか</strong></span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span><strong class="font-semibold">-te itadakemasen ka</strong></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> will generally sound better.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="question">❓</span> 先生もいらっしゃい<strong class="font-semibold">ませんか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sensei mo irasshai<strong class="font-semibold">masen ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t you join us, Professor?</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">It might seem polite enough, but it shows that the speaker is talking to the professor as if the professor is their equal.</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>先生もおい<strong class="font-semibold">でいただけませんか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sensei mo oi<strong class="font-semibold">de itadakemasen ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Could you please join us too, Professor?</div><div class="note text-sys-on-surface-variant" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">A humble expression</div></div></li></ul><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Check out our post on <a class="text-sys-primary underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity hover:opacity-80 break-words" href="/resources/learn/grammar/japanese/how-to-make-requests-in-japanese-">making requests in Japanese</a> to learn more!</p></div></div></aside></div></section><section class="section"><h3 class="text-balance text-headline-small text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="when-invitations-with-masen-ka-or-nai-ka-sound-unnatural">When invitations with 〜ませんか [masen ka] or 〜ないか [nai ka] sound unnatural</h3><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">Even though invitations that use negative questions are broadly more polite, there are some occasions where you cannot or should not issue an invitation ending with <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>. Let’s take a closer look!</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-disc [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-6 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> don’t sound natural when there is a prior <strong class="font-semibold">expectation </strong>that you and the listener would do something.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example:</strong> The teacher comes into the classroom and sees all the students at their seats.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>では、クラスを始め<strong class="font-semibold">ましょう</strong> / クラスを始め<strong class="font-semibold">ましょうか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Dewa, kurasu o hajime<strong class="font-semibold">mashō</strong> / kurasu o <strong class="font-semibold">mashō ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Well then, let’s start the class / shall we start the class?</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="question">❓</span>では、クラスを始め<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ませんか</span></strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Dewa, kurasu o hajime<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">masen ka</span></strong>.</div></div></li></ul></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> cannot be used to <strong class="font-semibold">prompt the listener to continue </strong>the act already started, as the example below. In such cases, we should use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Yoko and John are already on the way to a party when John suddenly stops and starts debating if going to the party is a good idea. Yoko prompts him to get going.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>何よ、今さら。さあ、行こ<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Nani yo, ima sara. Sā, ik<strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Too late for that. Now, let’s go.</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"> <span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="question">❓</span>さあ、行こ<strong class="font-semibold">うか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sā, ik<strong class="font-semibold">ō ka</strong>.<!-- --></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Now, shall we go?<!-- --></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span> さあ、行か<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ない？</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sā, ika<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">nai?</span></strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Now, why don’t we go?</div></div></li></ul></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> <strong class="font-semibold">won’t sound right when the situation is urgent</strong>. Use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)う</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> instead.</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>A group of friends are running through the airport, trying to catch a flight to Okinawa.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>飛行機出ちゃうよ。急ご<strong class="font-semibold">う</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Hikōki dechau yo. Isog<strong class="font-semibold">ō</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Our flight is gonna depart (without us). Let’s hurry.</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="question">❓</span>急ご<strong class="font-semibold">うか</strong>。 </div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Isog<strong class="font-semibold">ō ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Shall we hurry?<!-- --></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span> 急が<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ない？</span></strong></div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Isoga<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">nai?</span></strong></div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t we hurry?</div></div></li></ul></div></div></li><li class="py-1"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start"><span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-masen ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ないか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-nai ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> cannot be used <strong class="font-semibold">along with question words</strong>, to ask about a shared plan. In such cases, we need to use <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょうか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>-mashō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> or <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜(よ)うか</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>yō ka</span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span>:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en"><strong class="font-semibold">Example: </strong>Sachiko is at a bakery with her daughter to buy some sweets for the family gathering.</p><ul class="list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-none [&amp;&gt;li]:list-outside space-y-2 [&amp;&gt;li]:marker:text-foreground pl-0 [&amp;_ul]:pl-6 [&amp;_ol]:pl-6"><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="white_check_mark">✅</span>ドーナツ、いくつ買お<strong class="font-semibold">うか</strong>。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Dōnatsu, ikutsu ka<strong class="font-semibold">ō ka</strong>.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>How many doughnuts shall we buy?</div></div></li><li class="py-1"><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4 my-2" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase"><span class="not-italic" data-type="emoji" data-name="x">❌</span>ドーナツ、いくつ買わ<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">ない</span></strong>？</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Dōnatsu, ikutsu kawa<strong class="font-semibold"><span style="color:color-mix(in oklab, var(--mark-crimson) 88%, var(--pl-sys-on-surface))">nai</span></strong>?<!-- --></div></div></li></ul></div></div></li></ul></div></section></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="how-to-decline-an-invitation-in-japanese">How to decline an invitation in Japanese?</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">We’ve already seen several ways to respond positively to an invitation, but how should you decline one?</p><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start" lang="en">Paragraph contentIn Japanese, a straight “No” is considered rude, in a polite conversation. Instead, it’s better to use vague phrases such as <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">はちょっと...</span> <span class="gloss"><span aria-hidden="true">(</span><span class="transcription"><span class="sr-only">transcribed </span>wa chotto…</span><span aria-hidden="true">, </span><span class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">meaning </span><span aria-hidden="true">“</span>a little bit...<span aria-hidden="true">”</span></span><span aria-hidden="true">)</span></span></span> to politely decline an invitation. See an example below!</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div><div class="flex flex-col gap-3"><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Speaker A:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">土曜日、映画を見に行きませんか。</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Doyōbi, eiga o mi ni ikimasen ka.</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>Why don’t we go to see a movie on Saturday?</div></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-1"><span class="text-sm font-medium" style="color:var(--mark-ink)">Speaker B:</span><div class="single-phrase-usage-example flex flex-col gap-1 text-left px-3 py-2 bg-sys-surface-container-lowest rounded-r-md border-l-4" style="border-color:#EB321E"><div lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">すみません、土曜日<strong class="font-semibold">はちょっと</strong>・・・。　</div><div class="transcription text-sys-on-surface-variant"><span class="sr-only">Transcription: </span>Sumimasen, doyōbi <strong class="font-semibold">wa chotto</strong>….</div><div class="translation" lang="en"><span class="sr-only">Translation: </span>I’m sorry, Saturday is <strong class="font-semibold">a little bit</strong>…</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></section><section class="section"><h2 class="text-balance text-headline-large text-sys-on-surface font-sans scroll-mt-20" id="to-sum-up">To sum up</h2><div class="section-content"><p class="text-body-large text-sys-on-surface text-start">In this post, we have covered how to express invitation in Japanese, using three sentence patterns: <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょう /〜(よ)う</span></span>, <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ましょうか / 〜(よ)うか</span></span>, and <span class="inline-translation" style="color:var(--mark-ink)"><span lang="ja" dir="ltr" class="phrase">〜ませんか / 〜ないか</span></span>. Here&#x27;s a quick review table:</p><div class="indented ml-4"><div class="space-y-4"><div class="w-full"><div class="relative w-full overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full caption-bottom border-collapse text-base [&amp;_th]:h-10 [&amp;_th]:px-4 [&amp;_th]:py-2 [&amp;_td]:px-4 [&amp;_td]:py-3 table-auto"><caption class="mb-4 text-left text-sm text-muted-foreground"></caption><thead class=""><tr class="transition-colors hover:bg-muted/50 data-[state=selected]:bg-muted"><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle border-r border-b" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1"></th><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle bg-yellow-100 dark:bg-yellow-900 border-t border-r border-b border-l" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1">Volitional form</th><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle bg-yellow-100 dark:bg-yellow-900 border-t border-r border-b border-l" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1">Positive question</th><th class="h-10 px-4 py-2 font-medium text-left align-middle bg-yellow-100 dark:bg-yellow-900 border-t border-r border-b border-l" colSpan="1" rowSpan="1">Negative question</th></tr></thead><tbody class="[&amp;_tr: